---
title: The Design of Multi-Task King
blurb: How a board game about chasing dopamine got built, and what playtesters broke before it worked.
date: 2026-10-09
tags: [game]
---

Multi-Task King started life as a physical card game called Attentionality. The idea is this: you have one day, you have a limited amount of
attention, and you are trying to wring as much enjoyment out of that day as you
possibly can. The trouble is that the things most worth doing cost the most attention,
and the cheap hits that cost almost nothing are worth almost nothing.

## Two values per activity

Every activity in the game carries a duration and a dopamine value. Duration represents how much attention an activity takes to choose, 
and dopamine is what you score for choosing it. That's pretty much the whole
economy. You start the game with a mere five attention, with a cap of ten, 
which means a day is a short budget and you are constantly
deciding whether to spend it on one big thing or four less impactful ones.

If every card were just those two numbers, the game would be simple arithmetic. So most
cards do something in addition to their raw stats.

