Those three are representative of the kinds of cards in the deck. Get a Massage is a
multiplier, worth almost nothing by itself and enormous if you build a turn around
it. Go Shopping pays off only if you have already been busy, which rewards a plan
you committed to several decisions ago. Spread Rumors does nothing for your own
score and takes points off somebody else, which exists because a game where everyone
optimizes in parallel is not really a game.

There are a few other shapes in there. Effect cards can augment your attention at the end
of the day instead of paying in points, so Eat Fast Food is cheap and immediate but
leaves you with less ability tomorrow, and Meditate does the opposite, granting tons of attention at the cost of a lackluster turn. 
Gifting cards like Cook a Meal hand something to the next player, which matters more than it
seems it should.

## Roles pull you in a direction

At setup, each player picks a role out of three options, and the role usually pays a bonus
whenever you do either of two specific activities.

<div class="card-row">
	<img
		src="/multi-task-king/cinema-buff-bg.webp"
		alt="Cinema Buff role card: whenever you Watch a Film or Binge-Watch Shows, you get plus 3 dopamine"
		width="600"
		height="840"
		loading="lazy"
	/>
	<img
		src="/multi-task-king/easygoer-bg.webp"
		alt="Easygoer role card: whenever you Daydream or Get a Massage, you get plus 2 attention"
		width="600"
		height="840"
		loading="lazy"
	/>
	<img
		src="/multi-task-king/space-cadet-bg.webp"
		alt="Space Cadet role card: whenever you Smoke Up or Get Wasted, you get plus 2 attention"
		width="600"
		height="840"
		loading="lazy"
	/>
</div>

The two activities on a role are always thematically linked, which makes the card
easy to read and gives you an identity at the table. Cinema Buff wants screen time
and gets paid in points for it. Easygoer and Space Cadet both get paid in attention
instead, which is a different kind of bonus: it doesn't score anything directly, 
instead allowing for more explosive plays in the future.

The important part is that your role is a suggestion, not a plan. Only six activity
cards are face up at a time, so the thing your role loves may simply not be on the
table, and you have to decide whether to wait for it or take what is there.

## The penalty deck

At the end of each day, one penalty card is drawn and it applies to everybody. Each
one punishes a specific activity after the fact, so Brainrot docks you for every time
you scrolled shorts that day, Ragebait does the same for checking socials, and
One-Star does it for films. A couple of them target behavior rather than a specific
card, with Burnout hitting anyone who took two or more actions and Ennui hitting
anyone who took one or fewer.

The penalty deck is one of the things that prevents the game from being solvable. You cannot
plan around it, because you find out after you have already chosen the day's activities, and you
are stuck weighing whether a third helping of the activity you are good at is worth
the risk.

Originally, this was the single most over-tuned part of the game. The first version hit hard
enough that a bad draw could completely nullify a good day, and I got some pretty negative feedback
about it. Knocking a couple of points off every penalty kept the tension without
making luck of the draw feel like the whole game.

## What playtesting actually changed

The biggest and most recurring problem of the game was catching up to a runaway leader. 
Usually whoever got ahead early tended to stay ahead, due to a lack of meaningful catch-up mechanics. 
The fix was bandwagoning: if you take an action somebody already took this turn, you get a point for it and the person
who went first gets two, with more for each additional player who piles on. It gives
the trailing players a cheap way to stay close while making the leader's choices
public information worth copying, and it quietly turned a solitaire scoring race into
something you could strategize with each other during.

A smaller fix that was in the same spirit: a player sitting at four or fewer attention can
"sleep through the day", skipping their turn to get a whopping three attention back. Before that,
running yourself dry almost always meant a dead turn or two.

Playtesters also wanted the game to last longer and the deck to be less bland.
Rounds went from eight to ten, and the proportion of vanilla cards, the ones with no
text in the middle, came down in favor of cards that do something. One note I liked
was the request for a clearer split between devious and wholesome ways of getting
your dopamine, since Smoke Up and Call the Cops ought to feel different from Hit the
Gym and Cook a Meal even when the numbers are comparable.

## What the port changed

Building the Godot version surfaced a different category of problem, which is that
some cards were written in a way a person could follow but a computer could not,
and a few of those turned out to be confusing for people too.

Daydream originally gave you a point for every other one-point action you took. That
is a rule that makes you check the value of each card, apply the bonus, and then
wonder whether the bonus changed the value. I rewrote it to key off duration instead
of dopamine, which is a number that never moves. Get a Massage got a similar
treatment, going from doubling some of your actions to doubling all of them, with its
own base value dropped to one so the card stayed in line.

Drink Coffee is my favorite of these. It used to be an effect card that gave you
attention back at the end of the day. Now it just costs two less duration. Those are
the same thing from the player's side, and the second version needs no explanation
and no end of day bookkeeping.

A couple of changes were purely about overlap. Gamble Online and Eat Fast Food had
drifted into being the same card with different art, so Gamble Online moved up in
both cost and payoff to occupy a different slot. Drink Coffee went from two copies in
the deck to four once it was clear how often you want it.

## Where it stands

The digital version isn't quite a full game right now, due to me still needing to implement 'attack' activities among other things.
However, there *are* computer opponents included, and
you can customize the deck before you start. You could download the raw files from the <a href="https://github.com/BumbleBeing/Multi-Task-King">GitHub repo</a>, or 
the executable from the <a href="https://www.bumblebeing.com/projects/multi-task-king">project page</a>. 
What is left is mostly balance, which is the part that never really ends as it's beholden to my current design philosophy.

